    {
      "slug": "he-blocked-my-spot-for-a-year",
      "script": "Still in my spot? My condo included an assigned, painted-number space, but a neighbor's truck sat in it anyway for eleven months straight. He claimed he'd parked there before I bought the unit. Management mailed three written notices; nothing changed. I pulled the HOA bylaws and found unused numbered spots could be bought from the association—better, his own spot, two rows over, had sat empty. I bought it—not the space holding his truck, but his actual numbered spot. His truck got towed from both spaces in the same week, and he parks on the street now like the visitors he used to complain about. He left an angry note on my door, so I taped it to the new sign. He stole one spot, lost two, and still can't trust a curb?",
      "editor_note": "Compressed to 134 words, preserved every protected fact and shot phrase, and sharpened the two-space reversal into the specific curb question."
    },
    {
      "slug": "she-kept-taking-my-labeled-lunch",
      "script": "Still missing? My name had been on the container in the office fridge for three weeks, then someone started eating half of it every single day and returning the empty tub. I switched to a locked lunch bag. Gone again—bag and all. I bought the same yogurt, added two tablespoons of hot sauce only I could handle, and labeled it in huge letters: special diet, do not eat. Within an hour, our shared kitchen chat exploded with someone demanding to know who “poisoned” the yogurt. I said nothing. She confessed first, mid-sentence, before anyone asked her name. HR got involved, and a sign-out sheet appeared on the fridge door within a day. My lunch stayed untouched for two weeks straight. Turns out one honest label was enough?",
      "editor_note": "Compressed to 128 words, preserved every protected fact and shot phrase, and tightened the confession and honest-label payoff."
    }
  ],
